  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nNorthwest flow is occurring across the region this afternoon \nfollowing a cold frontal passage that occurred earlier today. In \nthis post-frontal environment, gusty winds have been ongoing and \nscattered to broken cloud cover has been occurring as lobes of \nvorticity at 500mb propagate through and low-level lapse rates \nremain moderately steep. An isolated sprinkle is not out of the \nquestion this afternoon, especially north of the Iron Range. \n\nThe strong winds being seen this afternoon are expected to ease \novernight, as high pressure gradually builds in from the west and a \nweak, shallow nocturnal inversion develops. One thing of note \novernight will be the potential for lake effect rain showers in the \nlee of large inland lakes near the International Border, such as \nLake of the Woods and Rainy Lake. The ongoing northwest winds will \nprovide CAA to the region into tonight, lowering 850mb temps to \nwithin a degree or two of 0 degC by early Sunday morning. With lake \nsfc temps still being in the 60s, hi-res model soundings suggest the \npotential for several hundred J/kg of lake induced CAPE to develop \nearly Sunday morning. In addition, the arrival of a 500mb shortwave \non early Sunday morning will help provide synoptic lift. This \nshortwave will aid in the development of lake effect rain showers, \nwhich was included in far northern MN where 850mb temps are \nexpected to be coldest.\n\nWith the exception of lake effect rain showers on Sunday morning \nnear the border, Sunday is shaping up to be a dry and sunny day \nacross the CWA. Temperatures will certainly feel much more like \nfall, with highs in the upper 50s to mid 60s. Northwest winds remain \nbreezy on Sunday, albeit a bit lighter than what is being observed \ntoday. On Sunday night, mostly clear to clear skies are expected to \ndrop temperatures into the 30s across a large portion of the CWA. \nPatchy frost will be possible in inland northwest WI and in the MN \nArrowhead. If forecast trends hold, Frost Advisories will likely be \nneeded. \n\nThe fall-like pattern is anticipated to change on Monday, as deep \nlow pressure propagates into the northern Great Plains and brings \nattendant sfc fronts through the CWA. With this weekend's high \npressure propagating downstream into the lower Great Lakes, this \nwill allow for southerly flow to advect Gulf moisture north into the \nCWA. PWATs of 1-2\" are possible, which would be in the 90th \npercentile or greater of climatology for the upper air site in \nInternational Falls. At this time, the ECMWF EFI is only showing QPF \nin the 60-70th percentile, which does decrease confidence in very \nheavy rainfall. The current QPF forecast maintains the NBM solution, \nwhich brings a broad 0.3 to 1.0\" across the CWA on Monday into \nMonday night. Heaviest rainfall is forecast over northwest \nWisconsin, where there is a Marginal risk for excessive rainfall and \njust a sliver of a Slight risk in southern Price County. Severe \nweather is unlikely as the better thermodynamic environment remains \nsouth of the area.   \n\nHigh pressure is favored to return following the early week low \npressure system, with a trend of warmer temps for the latter half of \nthe week. High temps from Tuesday into next weekend are forecast to \nbe largely in the 60s to low 70s. Additional rain chances arrive \nboth on Wednesday night into Thursday morning and again on Friday \ninto Saturday as flow aloft remains relatively zonal, allowing \nshortwaves to propagate through the Upper Midwest.",
